#' Synthetic baseline population for simeq examples
#'
#' A small, fully synthetic dataset of 2 000 simulated individuals designed
#' to illustrate the `simeq` simulation workflow without requiring access to
#' the confidential Portuguese National Health Survey (NHS) data.
#'
#' The dataset was generated by sampling ages from a Normal distribution
#' centred on 45 years (SD = 18), assigning sex with approximately equal
#' probability, drawing education level from age-dependent Bernoulli
#' probabilities, and drawing BMI from sex- and age-adjusted Normal
#' distributions with standard deviation 4.5 kg/m². No real individual
#' records are used.
#'
#' @format A tibble with 2 000 rows and 11 columns:
#' \describe{
#'   \item{person_id}{Character. Unique individual identifier (`synth_1`, etc.).}
#'   \item{age}{Integer. Age in years (20–84).}
#'   \item{sex}{Character. `"men"` or `"women"`.}
#'   \item{education_level}{Character. Two-category education: `"low"` or `"high"`.}
#'   \item{education_level2}{Character. Same as `education_level` (retained for
#'     compatibility with simulation functions that use both columns).}
#'   \item{survey_weight}{Numeric. Sampling weight (set to 1 for all synthetic
#'     individuals).}
#'   \item{bmi}{Numeric. Body mass index in kg/m² (rounded to 1 decimal place).}
#'   \item{weight}{Numeric. Body weight in kg, back-calculated from BMI and height.}
#'   \item{height}{Numeric. Height in metres (rounded to 3 decimal places).}
#'   \item{age_group}{Factor. Five-year age band (e.g., `[20,25)`, `[25,30)`, …).}
#'   \item{alive}{Logical. Alive status at baseline (`TRUE` for all individuals).}
#' }
#'
#' @source Generated synthetically; see `data-raw/create_synthetic_population.R`.
"simeq_synthetic_pop"
